{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And Moses spoke, etc., saying.</b> Behold, according to what has preceded us from their words, the expression of dibbur (speaking) indicates a harsh response. If so, why did the verse say <b>and Moses spoke before HaShem</b> — for this is not from the Mussar? Further, one needs to understand His saying <b>saying</b>, which has no meaning in what stands before us. And it is possible that Moses came with a claim of justice so as not to die, and not for what he was to be appointed to, but rather for Israel's sake, as I shall explain his words; therefore it used the expression of dibbur. And since in the words of Moses spoken on this matter they are nothing but words of love and compassion for Israel, the purpose of the words does not stand in what is explicitly said but in what is entailed by the statement — which is the prevention of death from him, as I shall explain — and that is His saying <b>saying.</b>"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:30.903262934Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}